@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Hit boxes") {
        The player can jump, but nothing happens when they land on a
        monster. Let's fix that by adding a **hit box** to the mob: a
        wide, thin
        [Area3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_area3d.html#class-area3d)
         that detects a stomp. When the player
        lands on it, we squash the mob and the player bounces off.

        We'll deal with the player getting hurt — the **hurt box** — in
        the next lesson. For now, the player is invincible; we just want
        the satisfying part, the squash.

        Putting the sensor on the mob keeps the player's code simple: the
        mob is in charge of its own life and death, and tells the rest of
        the game what happened through a signal.

        First, though, we need to set up physics layers so the right
        bodies listen to each other.
    }
    @Section(title: "Controlling physics interactions") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Physics bodies have access to two complementary properties: layers
            and masks. Layers define on which physics layer(s) an object is.

            Masks control the layers that a body will listen to and detect. This
            affects collision detection. When you want two bodies to interact,
            you need at least one to have a mask corresponding to the other.

            The important point is that you can use layers and masks to filter
            physics interactions, control performance, and remove the need for
            extra conditions in your code.

            By default, all physics bodies and areas are set to both layer and
            mask `1`. This means they all collide with each other.

            Physics layers are represented by numbers, but we can give them
            names to keep track of what's what.

            @Image(source: 07-01.physics-layers-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Let's give our physics layers a name. Tap the **Toolbar Menu**
                button in the upper-right corner and choose **Settings** to open Project
                Settings.

                @Image(source: 07-01a.open-project-settings.png)
            }
            @Step {
                In the left menu, navigate down to _Layer Names -> 3D Physics_.
                You can see a list of layers with a field next to each of them
                on the right. Name the first three layers _player_, _enemies_, and _world_, in that order.

                > Tip: Naming the layers is purely for the editor's benefit
                > — the layers themselves are still just integers. But using
                > readable names makes the _Collision_ section of the
                > _Inspector_ far easier to scan.

                @Image(source: 07-02.physics-layer-names.png)
            }

            Now, we can assign them to our physics nodes.
            @Step {
                In the _Main_ scene, select the `Ground` node. In the
                _Inspector_, tap to open the _Collision_ section. The ground is part of
                the world, so we want it to be part of the third layer. Tap the lit button to toggle **off** the
                first _Layer_ and toggle **on** the third one. Then, toggle
                **off** the _Mask_ by tapping on it.

                > Note: The _Mask_ property allows a node to
                > listen to interaction with other physics objects, but we
                > don't need it to have collisions. `Ground` doesn't need to
                > listen to anything; it's just there to prevent creatures
                > from falling.

                > Tip: You can tap the blue "List" button on the right side of
                > the properties to see a list of named layers.

                @Image(source: 07-03.ground-collision-settings.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Next up is the `Player`. Open `player.tscn` by double-tapping
                the file in the _File Tree_.

                Select the _Player_ node and set its _Collision -> Mask_ to
                both "enemies" and "world", deselecting Layer 1. You can leave the default _Layer_
                property as it is, because the first layer is the "player"
                layer.

                @Image(source: 07-04.player-physics-mask.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Then, open the _Mob_ scene by double-tapping on `mob.tscn`,
                select the `Mob` node, and set its _Collision -> Layer_ to "enemies". Unset its
                _Collision -> Mask_ entirely, leaving the mask empty.

                > Note: With an empty mask, the mob's `CharacterBody3D` itself won't
                > push or get pushed by anything — it just slides forward. All
                > the gameplay interactions (squashing and getting hit) will
                > happen through the `Area3D` sensors we add next. This keeps
                > the movement clean: mobs never get stuck on the player or on
                > each other.

                > Note: The mobs don't need to mask the "world" layer
                > because they only move on the XZ plane. We don't apply
                > any gravity to them by design.

                @Image(source: 07-05.mob-physics-mask.png)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Adding the hit box") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The hit box is the area that detects a stomp. We want it to
            cover the snake's body, so a wide, thin box works
            well. It should sit just above the model's back.

            @Image(source: 07-06.hit-box-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select the `Mob` node and add a child
                [Area3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_area3d.html#class-area3d).
                Name it `HitBox`. 
                
                Add a
                [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_collisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
                as a child of it.

                @Image(source: 07-07.add-hit-box-area.png, alt: "Add an Area3D named HitBox with a CollisionShape3D child to the Mob")
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Inspector_, assign a
                [BoxShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_boxshape3d.html#class-boxshape3d)
                to the _Shape_ property. Set its _Size_ to about `4` on X,
                `1` on Y, and `5` on Z — a wide, thin box.

                > Tip: The exact size isn't critical. You want it wide enough
                > that landing near the snake counts as a stomp, but thin
                > enough in height that the player only triggers it while
                > coming down on top. Tweak it when you test the game.
                
                @Image(source: 07-08.resize-hit-box-shape.png, alt: "Resize the BoxShape3D to a wide thin box above the snake's back")
            }
            @Step {
                Select the `HitBox` node and, in the _Signals_ tab, connect
                its `body_entered` signal to the `Mob` node's script.
                
                @Image(source: 07-09.connect-body-entered-signal.png, alt: "Connect the HitBox body_entered signal to the Mob")
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Coding the squash and the bounce") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Now we wire up the logic. The mob needs a `squashed` signal and
            a `squash()` function, plus the `body_entered` callback we just
            connected. The player needs a `bounce()` function. And `Main`
            needs to connect the mob's `squashed` signal so the squasher
            bounces.

            Let's start with the mob.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the `mob.gd` script. We'll add the `squashed` signal,
                the `squash()` function, and the callback for the hit box.

                The signal carries the body that did the squashing (`by`),
                so `Main` can make that body bounce. The callback only
                squashes the mob if the entering body is falling —
                `body.velocity.y < 0` — which means a player landing on top.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-05.gd, previousFile: mob-3d-04.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                Now open the `player.gd` script. We'll add the
                `bounce_impulse` property and the `bounce()` function.

                `bounce()` is called by `Main` after the player squashes a
                mob — it gives the player an upward impulse so they bounce
                off the snake's head.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-3d-09.gd, previousFile: player-3d-08.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                Finally, open the `main.gd` script. We connect the mob's
                `squashed` signal to a new `_on_mob_squashed(by)` function
                that calls `by.bounce()` if the squasher has a `bounce`
                method.
                
                We also add a `mob.squashed.connect()` line to bind that new function.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-03.gd, previousFile: main-3d-02.gd)
            }
            You can now run the game by tapping the **Play** button in
            the toolbar. Jump on a snake to squash it and bounce off it. The
            player can't die yet — that's the next lesson.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Code checkpoint") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Here are the complete scripts for the `Mob`, `Player`, and
            `Main` nodes at this point, for reference. You can use them to
            compare and check your code.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Starting with `mob.gd`.
                
                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-3d-05.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Next is `player.gd`.
                
                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-3d-09.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Finally, the `main.gd` script.
                
                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-03.gd, reset: true)
            }
            > Tip: If you copy any of this code directly and overwrite a 
            linked function, you will need to 
            link the signal again to ensure it works correctly.
            
            In the next lesson, we'll add the hurt box so the player can
            die — and make jumping over a snake a safe strategy.
        }
    }
}
